diff --git a/docs/victoriametrics/vmagent.md b/docs/victoriametrics/vmagent.md
index 87a5b27..982742f 100644
--- a/docs/victoriametrics/vmagent.md
+++ b/docs/victoriametrics/vmagent.md
@@ -967,6 +967,25 @@ to all the metrics scraped by the given `vmagent` instance:
 
 See also [how to shard data among multiple remote storage systems](#sharding-among-remote-storages).
 
+## Limiting the number of scrape targets per job
+
+Sometimes a scrape job backed by service discovery (Kubernetes, Consul, EC2, etc.) may unexpectedly return
+a huge number of targets because of a misconfiguration on the discovery side. Scraping all of these targets
+at once may spike memory and CPU usage and degrade scraping for other jobs running on the same `vmagent` instance.
+
+In order to protect against this, the `-promscrape.maxTargetsPerJob` command-line flag can be used for limiting
+the number of active scrape targets a single scrape job (`job_name`) can have at once, regardless of which
+service discovery mechanism(s) provided the targets. This limit can be overridden on a per-job basis with the
+`max_targets_per_job` option at `scrape_config` level. By default, there is no limit, so existing setups
+are unaffected unless this option is explicitly configured.
+
+If the number of discovered targets for a job exceeds the configured limit, then `vmagent` keeps scraping
+only up to the limit, while the excess targets are dropped and shown as `DROPPED` with the `target limit exceeded`
+reason at the `http://vmagent:8429/service-discovery` page, in the same way as targets dropped because of
+[relabeling](#relabeling-and-filtering) or [sharding](#scraping-big-number-of-targets). The number of currently
+excluded targets for a job is also exposed via the `vm_promscrape_scrape_pool_targets{scrape_job="<job_name>", status="excluded"}`
+metric.
+
 ## High availability
 
 It is possible to run multiple **identically configured** `vmagent` instances or `vmagent`
